Res-Q — how to work in this folder
==================================

Standing instructions for any AI agent (or person) picking this repository up. Read this
before doing anything else: it is the difference between a session that needs the same
brief typed out again and one that just works. Last updated 2026-10-12.

The two standing rules
----------------------

1. Always keep a visible todo list. Every request, however small, gets one; mark each item
   done as it lands rather than at the end. The user reads it as live progress.

2. Everything you change goes live by itself, and you must prove it. Nothing is left
   uncommitted, and "I pushed it" is not the finish line — "the live site is serving this
   commit" is. See *Verifying a change is live*, below.

Where the site actually lives
-----------------------------

Two hosts, one repository, and they deploy on different triggers:

    front-end  https://res-q-am16.vercel.app/     Vercel, root directory `frontend`,
                                                  deploys itself on every push (~15s)
    API        https://res-q-api.onrender.com     Render, `render.yaml`, rootDir `backend`,
                                                  Auto-Deploy set to **On Commit** in the
                                                  dashboard, so it also rebuilds on push

The Render service serves the pages as well, but the pages the user actually opens are
Vercel's. Both are fed by the same `frontend/` directory, so a page change ships twice and a
*backend* change only reaches the user's site once Render has rebuilt. The pages call the API
cross-origin through `frontend/resq_api.js` (`RESQ_API_BASE`), and Render's
`RESQ_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` lists the Vercel origin — an origin missing from that list is a CORS
refusal on every call, which reads like a broken page rather than a missing setting.

How "goes live" is wired
------------------------

    edit in this folder
      → committing pushes main by itself (a git post-commit hook, installed by
        scripts/autosync.sh --install)
      → Vercel rebuilds, and Render's Auto-Deploy rebuilds
      → .github/workflows/deploy.yml checks that the live API reports the pushed commit
        and warns (or fails, when it asked for the deploy itself) if it does not

* `scripts/autosync.sh` also contains a *watcher* that commits on its own, for a repository
  outside a macOS-guarded folder. **It cannot run here:** macOS refuses a launchd agent
  access to `~/Downloads` (that is the "Operation not permitted" in
  `/tmp/resq-autosync-launchd.log`), so in this checkout the commit hook is the mechanism
  and uncommitted edits wait for whoever made them. Move the repo out of `~/Downloads`, or
  grant Terminal and bash Full Disk Access, and `--install` will start the watcher too.
* The workflow needs no secret while Auto-Deploy is on. It takes `RENDER_DEPLOY_HOOK_URL`
  (or `RENDER_API_KEY` + `RENDER_SERVICE_ID`) as a fallback for a service that does not
  rebuild on commit — set it from a file, never in a shell history:

      gh secret set RENDER_DEPLOY_HOOK_URL < /path/to/hook.txt

Useful commands:

    scripts/autosync.sh --status      # is the automation installed
    scripts/autosync.sh --push        # push what is ahead, now
    scripts/autosync.sh --install     # (re)install the commit hook
    scripts/autosync.sh --uninstall   # stop it
    tail -f .git/autosync.log         # what it has been doing
    touch .git/autosync-off           # pause publishing; rm it to resume

Verifying a change is live
--------------------------

    git rev-parse --short=12 HEAD
    curl -s https://res-q-api.onrender.com/api/health

`/api/health` carries `revision` — the commit the answering process was built from (Render's
own `RENDER_GIT_COMMIT`, or the working copy locally). Those two shas must match for a
backend change. For a **page** change, check Vercel instead, because it deploys independently:

    curl -s https://res-q-am16.vercel.app/res_q_dashboard_donor.html | grep -c '<a string from your diff>'

A stale revision means a deploy that never happened, **not** a code bug — check the deploy
chain before touching the code. This cost a whole debugging session on 2026-10-12: three
commits including the donor dashboard's route map were pushed and correct while the live API
kept serving a build from days earlier, so `/api/post-claims` answered 404, the dashboard had
no claimants to route to, and the map panel sat on its drawn placeholder. It looked exactly
like a broken map.

**The map needs the API to be current.** `frontend/res_q_dashboard_donor.html` reads the
donor's claimed posts from `/api/post-claims` and the board from Supabase; with the claims
route missing it cannot draw a route at all.

Debugging the dashboard in a browser
-----------------------------------

* The route panel is `#orderRoutePanel`; its map is `#orderRouteMap`, mounted by MapLibre
  (`loadOrderRouteMap()` → `loadMaplibre()` → `mountOrderRouteMap()`), and its fallback
  drawing is the `#orderRouteRest` SVG laid over it. `paintOrderRoute()` is the one function
  that decides what the panel shows.
* **A browser that produces no animation frames cannot render that map at all.** In this
  app's Preview tab, `requestAnimationFrame` callbacks do not fire until a recording is
  started, and IntersectionObserver callbacks never fire — so the map looks permanently
  stuck there. `preview_recording_start` makes frames flow and everything then behaves.
* MapLibre's stylesheet makes every container `position: relative`, which beats Tailwind's
  `absolute inset-0`: a map whose box then collapses to zero height is invisible while
  looking perfectly mounted. `mountOrderRouteMap` pins the box inline for this reason.
* The route needs both ends to have confirmed pins. A claimant with no confirmed location is
  reported in the panel rather than drawn ("… has not confirmed a delivery location"), and
  the newest pending order on the demo board ("Tomatoes", claimed by a Fort York Food Bank
  contact) is exactly that — so the *default* selection shows that note instead of a map.
  Picking one of Ortega Deli's own three items draws the route.

The rationing pass
------------------

`backend/rationing.py` scores every claimant of a post and splits it. Two separate questions,
and using one answer for both was a bug:

* **the queue** is ranked by the whole documented score —
  `(distance_score * 0.3) + (need_score * 0.4) + (expiry_urgency_multiplier * 3.0)`, per
  `config.RATION_WEIGHTS`;
* **the split** — how much of the item each shelter gets — is the ratio of the two terms
  that are the claimant's own, distance and need, per `config.RATION_SHARE_WEIGHTS`
  (`rationing.share_weight`).

Urgency is read from the post, so it is the same number for every claimant of an item; a
constant added to every score before the scores are divided into each other survives the
division and drags every split toward an even one (Ortega Deli's three-way split came out
34.7/32.9/32.4 however differently the three shelters reported their need). After the fix the
demo splits 39.7/31.2/29.1, 56.1/43.9 and 57.9/42.1. If a split still reads too even, the
compression left is in `local_need_score`, whose floors (a 0.30 base and neutral-middle
defaults) cap how far apart three claimants can score.

Running it locally
------------------

    python3 backend/server.py            # pages + API on http://127.0.0.1:8080
    python3 backend/server.py 8123       # or on a port of your choosing

Check the port is free first (`lsof -i :8080`) — several agents share this checkout and a
second server on the same port looks like a site that will not load. Demo accounts, all with
the password `ResqDemo1!`:

    luis@resq.test    Ortega Deli, the Miami donor (the demo's subject)
    ana@resq.test     Biscayne Bay Shelter      ines@resq.test   Overtown Community Kitchen
    tomas@resq.test   Little Havana Food Pantry
    rosa@resq.test    dana@resq.test            noor@resq.test   (other fixture accounts)

`RESQ_PERSIST=off` (the default, and what the deployment runs) keeps accounts, sessions and
orders in the worker's memory — so **every Render deploy signs everybody out**, and a page
that suddenly redirects to the sign-in screen after a deploy is not a session bug.

Project conventions
-------------------

* Python 3 standard library only — no framework, no ORM. Routes live in
  `backend/server.py`; every constant and threshold lives in `backend/config.py`.
* Comments carry the *why*, at length, in full sentences. Match that voice; do not add
  banner comments or restate what the next line says.
* Frontend is single-file HTML with Tailwind from a CDN and one inline `<script>` — there is
  no build step and no bundler, so a change is a file edit. Vercel serves it in seconds.
* Long-lived facts about the deployment belong in `README.md`; this file is the short version
  for whoever is about to start work.
* Never commit a hook URL, an API key or a service id. `render.yaml` uses `sync: false` for
  the values that are somebody's own (origins, Supabase, the model key).

